  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low clouds and fog through Thursday morning. \n\nOnshore flow developing around high pressure to the north and\neast, as well as a weak warm front slowly lifting across the\nMid-Atlantic, will set the stage for increasing low-level\nmoisture. This results in the development of low clouds and fog\nfor much of the area, persisting into Thursday morning.\n\nFor today, low clouds likely linger through midday into early \nafternoon closer to the eastern (upslope) areas between the Blue\nRidge/Catoctin Mountains and US-15.\n\nWarm advection mid-level clouds may then persist much of the \nday as the weak warm front lifts north and moistening onshore \nflow continues. This is expected to keep temperatures a few\ndegrees cooler this afternoon when compared to the previous\ncouple of afternoons (i.e. upper 60s instead of lower 70s).\n\nLow clouds and some fog redevelop by late evening, persisting \ninto Thursday morning. Given a slightly more southeast (versus \neast) component to the low-level wind, this may allow low \nclouds/fog to expand further east to and across the I-95 \ncorridor. Southern Maryland to the Northern Neck of Virginia are\ncloser in proximity to the moisture sources of the Atlantic \nOcean, Chesapeake Bay and tidal Potomac River, and may therefore\nhave a chance at some drizzle by early Thursday.\n\nMore appreciable clearing is expected by mid/late Thursday \nmorning as winds shift to a bit west of south, helping to scour \nout some of the low-level moisture.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Well above normal temperatures Thursday and Friday\nafternoons, then more seasonable Saturday.\n\nFollowing a wind shift to the south and southwest on Thursday,\nwell above normal temperatures are expected. Afternoon high \ntemperatures reach into the lower to middle 70s. A dry front\nlooks to cross the area Friday turning winds to the northwest,\nthough I hesitate to call this a cold front with the lack of\ncold air advection in its wake. The airmass will be drier, and\nthat coupled with downsloping flow is expected to push high\ntemperatures well into the middle to upper 70s on Friday. While\nthis is a solid 8 to 15 degrees above normal for late October, \nit is still several degrees shy of daily records.\n\nSome cold advection does take hold Friday night in the wake of a\nsecondary boundary as winds shift to the north. This should\nallow for low temperatures to fall into the upper 30s and 40s.\n\nA combination of subtle cold air advection and increased clouds\nahead of troughing developing over the middle of the country \nwill result in more seasonable daytime high temperatures on \nSaturday - generally in the 60s (50s mountains). Anticipate lows\ndropping into the 40s Saturday night with a continued increase \nin clouds but near zero chance of precipitation.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...On-and-off rain showers are possible beginning \nSunday into early next week.\n\nA broad area of low pressure will form over the Midwest, and \nthen track east late in the weekend. There is a potential for \nrain showers beginning on Sunday, though it has trended drier \nover the past several model runs. Mentionable precipitation \nchances now hold off until Sunday evening in the latest forecast\npackage. These chances linger into Monday. Temperatures remain \nin the upper 60s for highs Sunday and Monday (50s mountains), \nnear to slightly above average for the start of November.\n\nAs the initial system tracks offshore by Tuesday, a second, \npotentially more potent upper-level low could form over the \nPlains and make its way to the region by the middle of next \nweek. Uncertainty remains high regarding the track of this \nsystem, but this could bring additional rainfall to the region.",
